.\" Process this file with
.\" groff -man -Tascii timer_create.2
.\"
.TH TIMER_CREATE 2 "July 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "System Calls Manual"
.SH NAME
timer_create \- create a POSIX per-process interval timer
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library (libc, -lc)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <signal.h>
.br
.B #include <time.h>
.PP
.BI "int timer_create(clockid_t " clockid ", struct sigevent *" sevp ,
.BI "                 timer_t *" timerid ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR timer_create ()
creates a new per-process interval timer, initially disarmed, and stores its
identifier in
.IR *timerid .
The timer is armed and queried with
.BR timer_settime (2)
and
.BR timer_gettime (2).
.PP
The
.I clockid
argument selects the clock the timer is measured against.  Substrate
supports:
.TP
.B CLOCK_REALTIME
A settable, system-wide wall-clock timer.
.TP
.B CLOCK_MONOTONIC
A non-settable clock that measures time since an unspecified epoch and is not
affected by changes to the wall clock.
.PP
The
.I sevp
argument points to a
.B struct sigevent
that specifies how the caller is notified when the timer expires:
.TP
.B SIGEV_SIGNAL
The signal
.I sevp\->sigev_signo
is generated for the process on each expiration.  If a handler is installed
with
.BR SA_SIGINFO ,
the accompanying
.B siginfo_t
has
.I si_code
set to
.B SI_TIMER
and
.I si_value
set to
.IR sevp\->sigev_value .
.TP
.B SIGEV_NONE
No notification is delivered; the timer may still be queried with
.BR timer_gettime (2).
.PP
If
.I sevp
is NULL, the timer behaves as if
.I sigev_notify
were
.BR SIGEV_SIGNAL ,
.I sigev_signo
were
.BR SIGALRM ,
and
.I sigev_value
were the timer id.
.PP
Timers are
.B not
inherited by a child created with
.BR fork (2)
and are disarmed and deleted upon
.BR execve (2).
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success,
.BR timer_create ()
returns 0 and the timer id is written to
.IR *timerid .
On error, \-1 is returned and
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
The clock id
.I clockid
is not supported, or the requested
.I sigev_notify
(for example
.BR SIGEV_THREAD ,
which is realized in the C library) is not supported by the kernel, or
.I sevp\->sigev_signo
is not a valid signal.
.TP
.B EAGAIN
The per-process timer table is full (the kernel could not allocate a new
timer).
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I sevp
or
.I timerid
points outside the accessible address space.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
struct sigevent ev;
timer_t tid;
struct itimerspec its;

ev.sigev_notify = SIGEV_SIGNAL;
ev.sigev_signo  = SIGALRM;
if (timer_create(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &ev, &tid) != 0)
        perror("timer_create");

its.it_value.tv_sec = 2;     its.it_value.tv_nsec = 0;
its.it_interval.tv_sec = 0;  its.it_interval.tv_nsec = 0;
timer_settime(tid, 0, &its, NULL);
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR clock_gettime (2),
.BR timer_settime (2),
.BR timer_gettime (2),
.BR timer_delete (2),
.BR timer_getoverrun (2),
.BR sigaction (2),
.BR sigevent (3type)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2008.
.SH NOTES
Timer expiry is evaluated against the kernel tick, so the effective
resolution is bounded by the system clock granularity (see
.BR clock_getres (3)).
